WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.200
- [Narrator] If you're looking to save

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.670
as much money as possible

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.590
when filing your taxes this year,

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:06.990
knowing this one number will help.

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:10.020
Your adjusted gross income or AGI.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.300
- Adjusted gross income
is the starting point

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.410
for your tax return.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:18.060
It's a common measure of
income that the IRS uses

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:22.200
and the government uses to
determine how much money

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.540
you've made over the
course of the past year.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.760
- [Narrator] So to
demystify your tax return,

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.340
we break down how to calculate your AGI

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.085
and how this one number
impacts your finances.

00:00:33.085 --> 00:00:35.668
(mellow music)

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:40.410
The IRS defines adjusted
gross income, or AGI,

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:43.740
as your gross income minus
adjustments to income.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.950
It's commonly referred to as the line.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.410
Above the line, you have two things.

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.810
Your gross income and your adjustments.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:55.050
Your gross income is a
combination of your wages,

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.570
stock dividends, capital gains

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:01.290
and any other income you might
have, like business profits.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:02.760
Adjustments refer to things

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.920
like interest on student loan payments,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.290
contributions to some retirement accounts

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:10.920
and expenses you may have as
a teacher, just to name a few.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.080
These adjustments are commonly referred to

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.630
as above the line deductions.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:18.030
And once you subtract them
from your gross income,

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:19.443
you've got your AGI.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:24.270
So once you've calculated
it, why does it matter?

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:27.780
Let's take a look at Olivia
and calculate her gross income.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:32.430
Olivia earned $60,000 a year
from her day job as a chemist.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.070
After subtracting her 401K contributions

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.250
and health insurance
premiums, which aren't taxed,

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:42.090
her taxable wage is $57,000 a year.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.340
She also rented out her extra bedroom,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.130
which brought in a net total of $6,000.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.710
And lastly, Olivia bought and sold stocks

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.800
within the past year, profiting $1000.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.350
Because she held the stocks
for only a few months,

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.320
her profit is considered
a short term capital gain

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.720
and is taxed at the ordinary income rate,

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.390
meaning it's less
complicated for our purposes.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:08.390
So all together, Olivia's gross
income comes out to $64,000.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:11.550
But remember, if you have
other sources of income,

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.980
like from freelancing or side hustles,

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.950
you'll need to add those to
your gross income as well.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.020
Now, to find Olivia's AGI,

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:22.500
she has to subtract the
adjustments from her gross income.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.960
There are quite a few possibilities here

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.350
like educator expenses and
self-employed health insurance.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:31.650
One of those adjustments is
interest on student loan debt.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.720
Olivia has private student loan debt

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.670
and made payments throughout the year,

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.850
so she can subtract up to $2,500,

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:42.600
bringing her AGI to $61,500.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.580
This brings us back to why knowing

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.010
your adjusted gross income matters.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.050
After your AGI has been calculated,

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:52.410
you can check for any tax
credits you may qualify for.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.750
- Adjusted gross income
is used by Congress

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.720
to set parameters for various tax breaks

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.060
to basically ensure that they're going to

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.710
lower and middle income people

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:05.040
and then aren't fully available
to very high income people.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.680
- [Narrator] There are a lot
of tax credits available.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.800
- For example, you have something
called the saver's credit

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.900
which is essentially a government match

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.120
for retirement contributions

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:16.500
and you have the earned income credit

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.210
which is the government's main program

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:21.030
for assisting low and
middle income families.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:21.863
- [Narrator] Last year,

00:03:21.863 --> 00:03:24.450
31 million eligible workers and families

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:27.090
benefited from the
earned income tax credit,

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.090
averaging savings of more than $2,000.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:31.590
There are also tax credits

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:35.010
with eligibility tied to AGI
for students and parents,

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.230
like the American Opportunity Tax Credit

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:38.610
or Child Tax Credit.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:40.830
But these situations
don't apply to Olivia,

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.020
so she doesn't get to claim tax breaks

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:45.090
based on AGI this year.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.010
After you calculate your tax credits,

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:50.430
you can take deductions and
determine your actual tax bill.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.400
- The more you understand
about how the tax code works,

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.170
how it keys off of this number,

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:57.210
the easier it can be to figure out

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:00.090
why you're getting to the bottom
line number you're getting.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:01.410
- [Narrator] If you hire an accountant

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.090
or use other tax services,

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:05.400
you may not need to calculate your AGI

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.320
to figure out how to save with it.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.780
But no matter whether your
taxes are in your hands

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:12.750
or someone else's, this
little number on line 11

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.123
plays a key role in your financial life.

